WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:06.200 align:start position:0%
Why do trophy trucks use bodies that
resemble trucks from well-known brands?

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:10.800 align:start position:0%
As it turns out, it's not just a matter
of design or a love for a particular

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:14.900 align:start position:0%
brand. In races like the Baja 1000,
trophy trucks are subject to rules

00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:18.700 align:start position:0%
requiring them to retain an appearance
similar to that of production trucks or

00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:22.800 align:start position:0%
SUVs. [music] However, don't be fooled
by their exterior. These vehicles are

00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:27.700 align:start position:0%
actually built almost entirely from
scratch using a custom racing tubular

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:31.900 align:start position:0%
frame. In fact, the body isn't made of
factory standard panels, but rather from

00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:35.800 align:start position:0%
lightweight materials like fiberglass or
carbon fiber. Grilles, headlight

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:40.200 align:start position:0%
designs, and body lines characteristic
of each manufacturer make it easy for

00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:44.100 align:start position:0%
spectators and fans to recognize. These
truck body regulations serve to

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:48.100 align:start position:0%
distinguish the trophy truck class from
open wheel classes like Class 1 Buggy.

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:53.000 align:start position:0%
So, even though it looks like a Ford
F-150, what you're actually seeing is a

00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:56.900 align:start position:0%
specialized race car that merely borrows
the visual identity of a production

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:56.900 align:start position:0%
truck.

